# ============================================================================
# Network Module - Main Configuration
# ============================================================================
#
# PURPOSE:
#   This is a Terraform module that encapsulates network infrastructure configuration.
#   Modules are reusable components that can be called from other Terraform
#   configurations. This module handles all networking resources (VPC/VNet, subnets,
#   security groups, etc.) in a provider-agnostic way.
#
# KEY CONCEPTS FOR BEGINNERS:
#   - Modules: Reusable Terraform configurations that can be called like functions
#   - Module structure: Similar to root modules (variables, resources, outputs)
#   - Module benefits: Reusability, organization, abstraction
#   - Module usage: Called from root module using module blocks
#   - Provider abstraction: This module works across AWS, Azure, and GCP
#
# TERRAFORM DOCUMENTATION:
#   - Modules: https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/modules
#   - Module Composition: https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/modules/develop
#   - Module Structure: https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/modules/develop/structure
#
# HOW MODULES WORK:
#   1. This module defines inputs (variables) and outputs
#   2. Root modules call this module: module "network" { source = "./modules/network" }
#   3. Root modules pass values to module variables
#   4. Module creates resources and returns outputs
#   5. Root modules can use module outputs (e.g., VPC ID, subnet IDs)
# ============================================================================

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NETWORK INFRASTRUCTURE CONFIGURATION
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This module would contain all networking resources:
# - VPC/VNet creation and configuration
# - Subnets (public and private) across availability zones
# - Internet Gateway / NAT Gateway (for public internet access)
# - Security Groups / Network Security Groups (for access control)
# - Route Tables (for network routing)
# - VPC Endpoints / Private Endpoints (for private service access)
#
# The actual implementation would vary based on the cloud provider:
# - AWS: Uses aws_vpc, aws_subnet, aws_internet_gateway, aws_security_group
# - Azure: Uses azurerm_virtual_network, azurerm_subnet, azurerm_network_security_group
# - GCP: Uses google_compute_network, google_compute_subnetwork, google_compute_firewall
#
# Example structure (provider-specific):
# # AWS example:
# resource "aws_vpc" "this" {
#   cidr_block = var.vpc_cidr
#   # ... configuration
# }
#
# # Azure example:
# resource "azurerm_virtual_network" "this" {
#   name                = "${var.project_prefix}-vnet"
#   address_space       = [var.vpc_cidr]
#   # ... configuration
# }
#
# # GCP example:
# resource "google_compute_network" "this" {
#   name                    = "${var.project_prefix}-vpc"
#   auto_create_subnetworks = false
#   # ... configuration
# }

# Note: This is a module placeholder. The actual resources would be configured
# based on the cloud provider (AWS, Azure, or GCP) and specific requirements.
